Auto-instrumentation handles HTTP calls and database queries. But when a critical business operation — payment processing, fraud scoring, inventory reservation — happens within a single service, you want its own span in the trace so you can see how long it took and whether it failed.

Create custom spans with the Micrometer `Tracer` API. The try-with-resources pattern ensures the span is always closed, even if the operation throws.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final Tracer tracer;

    public PaymentResult processPayment(Order order) {
        Span paymentSpan = tracer.nextSpan().name("payment-gateway-call");
        try (Tracer.SpanInScope ws = tracer.withSpan(paymentSpan.start())) {
            paymentSpan.tag("order.id", order.getId());
            paymentSpan.tag("amount", order.getTotal().toString());
            PaymentResult result = gateway.charge(order);
            paymentSpan.tag("payment.status", result.getStatus());
            return result;
        } catch (PaymentException e) {
            paymentSpan.error(e);  // marks span as failed in trace UI
            throw e;
        } finally {
            paymentSpan.end();
        }
    }
}
```

Async boundaries — Kafka, RabbitMQ, `@Async` thread pool hand-offs — are where traces silently break. When the order service publishes an event to Kafka, the consumer runs in a different service on a different thread, with no HTTP headers to extract context from. Spring Cloud Stream with Micrometer handles this automatically for `@StreamListener` and functional consumers, but custom Kafka producers need manual injection.

```java
Span current = tracer.currentSpan();
if (current != null) {
    ProducerRecord<String, OrderEvent> record =
        new ProducerRecord<>("order-events", event);
    tracing.propagation().injector(ProducerRecord::headers)
        .inject(current.context(), record);
    kafkaTemplate.send(record);
}
```

Without this injection, the consumer's processing appears as an orphaned trace with no connection to the originating request. You can't see end-to-end latency from the user's click to the notification being sent.

**Never trace 100% of requests in production.** Tracing adds serialization, network traffic to the collector, and storage cost. A high-traffic service at 10,000 req/s generates 10,000 spans per second at 100% sampling — overwhelming Zipkin's storage. `probability: 0.1` (10%) captures enough traffic to understand behavior without the cost.

The gap in probabilistic sampling: low-frequency errors. A 0.1% error rate at 10% sampling means 1 in 1000 error requests is traced. For debugging production incidents, that's often insufficient. Implement a custom `Sampler` that forces sampling for any request that results in a 5xx response:

```java
@Bean
Sampler errorForcingSampler() {
    return (traceContext, span) -> {
        // Force sampling for error spans regardless of global probability
        if (span.tags().containsKey("error")) return SamplingFlags.SAMPLED;
        return SamplingFlags.NOT_SAMPLED_OR_PARENT; // use global rate
    };
}
```

**Custom spans** add business context that auto-instrumentation cannot provide. Knowing a payment processing span took 4 seconds is useful. Knowing it was processing a $50,000 transaction is more useful for prioritization.

```java
@Autowired Tracer tracer;

Span span = tracer.nextSpan().name("process-payment").start();
try (Tracer.SpanInScope scope = tracer.withSpan(span)) {
    span.tag("payment.amount", amount.toString());
    span.tag("payment.currency", currency);
    span.tag("payment.method", paymentMethod);
    return paymentGateway.charge(amount, currency);
} catch (Exception e) {
    span.error(e);
    throw e;
} finally {
    span.end();
}
```

With `payment.amount` as a span tag in Zipkin, you can filter: "show me all traces where payment amount > $10,000 that took longer than 3 seconds." This is observability with business context — the difference between "something was slow" and "high-value transactions are slow."
